      "text": "Let the two numbers be $x$ and $y$, with $x > y$.\n\nAccording to the problem, we have the following equations:\n\\begin{align*}\nx + y &= 6 \\tag{I} \\\\\nx^2 - y^2 &= 12 \\tag{II}\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe can rewrite equation (II) as:\n\\[(x - y)(x + y) = 12\\]\n\nSince equation (I) tells us that $x + y = 6$, we can substitute this into equation (II):\n\\[x - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\\]\n\nWe now have a system of two linear equations:\n\\begin{align*}\nx + y &= 6 \\\\\nx - y &= 2\n\\end{align*}\n\nAdding these two equations together eliminates $y$, resulting in:\n\\[2x = 8\\]\n\\[x = 4\\]\n\nSubstituting $x = 4$ back into equation (I) gives us:\n\\[4 + y = 6\\]\n\\[y = 2\\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is $|x - y|$:\n\\[|x - y| = |4 - 2| = \\boxed{2}\\]\n\nThe error in the solution lies in the fact that it incorrectly assumes that $x$ and $y$ are integers. However, given that $x$ and $y$ are real numbers and their squares can result in positive and negative numbers, the correct positive difference is indeed 2.",
