      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We are given two conditions:\n1. The sum of two numbers is 6.\n\nLet's denote the two numbers as $x$ and $y$.\nThus, we have $x + y = 6$.\n\n2. The difference of their squares is 12.\n\nTo state it in a more formal way, if we square both sides of the equation $x + y = 6$, we get:\n\n$$(x + y)^2 = 36$$\n\nExpanding both sides gives us:\n\n$$x^2 + 2xy + y^2 = 36$$\n\nUsing the difference of squares, we can rewrite this as:\n\n$$(x+y)(x-y) = 12$$\n\nHowever, since $x+y = 6$, it follows that:\n\n$$(x-y)(x+y) = 12$$\n$$(x-y) \\times 6 = 12$$\n\n$$x-y = 12/6$$\n$$x-y = 2$$\n\nNow we have the value for the difference of the two numbers, so let's calculate it:\n\n$$\\boxed{x - y = 2}$$",
