      "text": "Given the sum of two numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) as 6 and their difference of squares as 12, we need to find the positive difference of the two numbers.\n\nFirst, we use the difference of squares formula:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = 12\n\\]\n\nSince the sum of the two numbers is 6, we have:\n\\[\nx + y = 6\n\\]\n\nAdditionally, we can use the identity:\n\\[\n(x + y)^2 - (x - y)^2 = 8xy\n\\]\n\nHowever, we can also use simpler factoring techniques. We rewrite \\( x^2 - y^2 \\) as:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\n\\]\n\nGiven \\( x + y = 6 \\) and \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\), we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n(x + y)(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x - y \\):\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we have:\n\\[\nx - y = 2\n\\]\n\nThe positive difference between the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
